util.c: Perl_repeatcpy copies each item with memcpy, not byte by byte.

The list form of x (pp_repeat, OPpREPEAT_DOLIST) repeats the stack's SV pointers
through repeatcpy, whose first PERL_REPEATCPY_LINEAR copies were a byte loop.
A byte copy drops a capability's tag: the repeated SV pointers are untagged and
the first use faults, cause 24 in pp_mapwhile for 'map chop(+()), ("")x20' or
'map { $_ } (1) x 100000' (found on SPEC's perlbench, op/chop.t, 2026-10-15).
The doubling copies after it already use memcpy. Behaviour unchanged elsewhere.

--- a/util.c
+++ b/util.c
@@ -3558,10 +3558,10 @@
 
         linear = count < PERL_REPEATCPY_LINEAR ? count : PERL_REPEATCPY_LINEAR;
         for (items = 0; items < linear; ++items) {
-            const char *q = from;
-            IV todo;
-            for (todo = len; todo > 0; todo--)
-                *p++ = *q++;
+            /* capstone: memcpy, not a byte loop: the list form of x copies SV
+               pointers, and a byte copy drops a capability's tag. */
+            memcpy(p, from, len);
+            p += len;
         }
 
         half = count / 2;
